<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Web">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <RootNamespace>EntKube.TelemetryNode</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>entkube-telemetry</AssemblyName>
    <InvariantGlobalization>true</InvariantGlobalization>

    <!--
      Workstation GC, deliberately, on a web SDK project that would otherwise get Server GC.

      Server GC keeps one heap and one budget PER CORE and returns memory to the OS reluctantly by
      design — it trades footprint for throughput. That is the right trade for the management plane's
      web front end and the wrong one here: this pod is a memory-bound indexer sharing a node with the
      workloads it observes, its ceiling is a cgroup limit rather than the machine, and on a 16-core
      node Server GC turns a modest live set into an RSS several times larger and then holds it. The
      indexing work is not throughput-bound enough to notice the difference; the memory limit is.

      Set DOTNET_gcServer=1 in the pod to put it back on a node with cores to spare and RAM to burn.
    -->
    <ServerGarbageCollection>false</ServerGarbageCollection>
    <ConcurrentGarbageCollection>true</ConcurrentGarbageCollection>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    The in-cluster telemetry node: one binary, two roles.

      indexer  — receives OTLP from the cluster's collector, owns the hot active index and the warm
                 sealed segments on its PersistentVolume, seals to object storage.
      querier  — answers queries from the management plane, reading cold segments from object storage
                 into a bounded local cache, and the hot/warm ones from the indexer.

    Deployed as two catalog components off this same image, so read and write scale independently and a
    heavy query can never stall ingest. See docs/telemetry-in-cluster.md.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- The node keeps its segment catalog in a SQLite file on its own volume. Raw ADO.NET, not EF:
         the catalog is four statements and the engine is deliberately EF-free. -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Data.Sqlite" Version="10.0.1" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\EntKube.Telemetry\EntKube.Telemetry.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <Using Include="EntKube.Telemetry" />
    <Using Include="EntKube.Web.Data" />
    <Using Include="EntKube.Web.Services" />
    <!-- Same collision as in the engine: this assembly means log severity, not logger verbosity. -->
    <Using Include="EntKube.Web.Services.LogLevel" Alias="LogLevel" />
  </ItemGroup>


  <!--
    CVE-2025-6965 (GHSA-2m69-gcr7-jv3q, high): SQLitePCLRaw.lib.e_sqlite3 <= 2.1.11 ships a
    vulnerable SQLite, and it arrives here transitively through Microsoft.Data.Sqlite. The advisory
    records no patched package version and the nuspecs name no SQLite version, so the versions were
    read out of the shipped native libraries rather than inferred:

        2.1.11 -> SQLite 3.49.1   (vulnerable; the fix is in 3.50.2)
        2.1.14 -> SQLite 3.54.0   (fixed)
        3.50.3 -> SQLite 3.50.3   (fixed, but the 3.x line renumbers the package to track SQLite)

    2.1.14 keeps the native library on the same package line as the managed SQLitePCLRaw.core and
    bundle that Microsoft.Data.Sqlite depends on, so nothing else has to move.

    This bump is what exposed the AlertRoutingRules rebuild in
    20260611122519_AddClusterServers (SQLite); the two changes belong together, because the pin
    without that repair breaks every fresh SQLite install.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="SQLitePCLRaw.lib.e_sqlite3" Version="2.1.14" />
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>
